    "operation": "review",
    "state": "final",
    "summary": "Reviewed the single added file examples/acceptance/bounds.mjs. The `at` function's bounds guard has an off-by-one error: it tests `index > values.length`, which allows `index === values.length` to pass the guard and return `values[values.length]` (i.e. `undefined`) instead of throwing RangeError as the documented contract requires.",
    "findings": [
      {
        "title": "Off-by-one in array bounds check allows index equal to length",
        "body": "The function contract states it throws RangeError outside the array, but valid nonnegative indices of an array with length n are 0 through n-1. The guard `index > values.length` uses a strict greater-than, so `at(values, values.length)` is treated as in-bounds and returns `undefined` rather than throwing. The check should be `index >= values.length`.",
        "severity": "medium",
        "category": "correctness",
        "confidence": 0.99,
        "path": "examples/acceptance/bounds.mjs",
        "line": 3,
        "evidence": "Guard reads `if (!Number.isInteger(index) || index < 0 || index > values.length)`; at index === values.length the expression `index > values.length` is false, so `return values[index]` executes and yields undefined.",
        "suggestion": "Change the comparison to `index >= values.length` so that index equal to the array length throws RangeError."
      }
    ],
    "diagnosis": "The guard compares against the array length with a strict greater-than operator, but the largest valid index is values.length - 1, so the out-of-bounds value values.length is not rejected."
  },
